1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,040
This video is about L L one grammars. These are the grammars that a recursive descent parser can use.

2
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:26,221
We learn two simple tests that show a grammar is not L L one: common prefixes and left recursion. We learn how to refactor the grammar to remove both problems, and how to convert E B N F to B N F. Then we solve three exercises from the course notes.

3
00:00:26,671 --> 00:00:40,772
The course notes study three classes of grammars. The largest is the context free grammars. A general parser for them takes order n cubed time, and needs a nondeterministic pushdown automaton.

4
00:00:41,222 --> 00:00:53,489
Inside it is L L one. We can write parsers for these grammars by hand, with recursive descent. They parse in linear time, with a deterministic pushdown automaton.

5
00:00:53,939 --> 00:01:00,445
Inside L L one are the regular languages, which we recognize with finite automata.

6
00:01:00,895 --> 00:01:07,850
The question for this video and the next one is: is a given grammar inside L L one, or outside?

7
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:15,255
L L one is short for three things. The first L: the parser reads the input from left to right.

8
00:01:15,705 --> 00:01:23,107
The second L: it builds a leftmost derivation. It always expands the leftmost nonterminal first.

9
00:01:23,557 --> 00:01:29,509
And 1: it looks at one token of lookahead. That is the next token, not yet consumed.

10
00:01:29,959 --> 00:01:37,234
Here the parser has read i d and the arrow. It now expects a Factor. Factor has four alternatives.

11
00:01:37,684 --> 00:01:50,719
The lookahead is the not sign. Only the first alternative can start with it. So the parser picks it, with no guessing. That is the L L one property. One token predicts the alternative.

12
00:01:51,169 --> 00:01:58,955
A recursive descent parser has one function per nonterminal. Here is a sketch for Factor. It is not lab code.

13
00:01:59,405 --> 00:02:06,211
The first test looks at the next token. If it is the not sign, the parser takes the first alternative.

14
00:02:06,661 --> 00:02:11,098
If it is an open parenthesis, it takes the second alternative.

15
00:02:11,548 --> 00:02:14,343
If it is an identifier, it calls Var.

16
00:02:14,793 --> 00:02:28,979
Otherwise it must be a constant. Each test looks at one token, and the tests must not overlap. When one token can start two alternatives, the parser cannot choose. Then the grammar is not L L one.

17
00:02:29,429 --> 00:02:41,355
The notes list three things that put a grammar outside L L one. The first is common prefixes: two alternatives of the same nonterminal that start with the same symbols.

18
00:02:41,805 --> 00:02:47,074
The second is left recursion: a production for A whose right side starts with A.

19
00:02:47,524 --> 00:02:53,327
The third is ambiguity: a string with two parse trees. Video 18 covered it.

20
00:02:53,777 --> 00:03:08,753
The two simple tests can only show that a grammar is not L L one. After we refactor, the new grammar might be L L one. To know for sure, we need the full test with the predict sets. That is video 19 b.

21
00:03:09,203 --> 00:03:21,256
Here is the example from the notes. A statement is an assignment, i d colon equals expression, or a call, i d, open parenthesis, expression, close parenthesis.

22
00:03:21,706 --> 00:03:25,439
The parser expects a statement. The lookahead is i d.

23
00:03:25,889 --> 00:03:32,119
Both alternatives start with i d. One token of lookahead cannot choose between them.

24
00:03:32,569 --> 00:03:41,785
The tokens that decide are colon equals or the open parenthesis. They are the second token. So this grammar is not L L one.

25
00:03:42,235 --> 00:03:51,920
The fix is called left factoring. Here is the general pattern from the notes. Goal has two alternatives, and both start with the same Prefix.

26
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:57,533
We keep one copy of the Prefix, and follow it with a new nonterminal, Tail.

27
00:03:57,983 --> 00:04:01,289
Tail has the two suffixes as its alternatives.

28
00:04:01,739 --> 00:04:13,729
Now the parser reads the shared prefix first. It chooses between the suffixes only after the prefix, when the next token is different. The new grammar accepts the same strings.

29
00:04:14,179 --> 00:04:28,301
Apply the pattern. The prefix is i d. The new grammar is: statement goes to i d Tail. Tail goes to colon equals expression, or to open parenthesis, expression, close parenthesis.

30
00:04:28,751 --> 00:04:35,983
Parse i d, colon equals, x. Statement has only one alternative. The parser reads i d.

31
00:04:36,433 --> 00:04:42,748
Now it expects a Tail. The lookahead is colon equals, so it picks the assignment alternative.

32
00:04:43,198 --> 00:04:56,425
The two Tail alternatives start with different tokens, colon equals and open parenthesis. A check in python found no overlap, and both grammars give the same strings up to length 7.

33
00:04:56,875 --> 00:05:10,933
Now the second problem. A list of identifiers, separated by commas. List goes to List comma i d, or to i d. The first alternative starts with List itself. That is left recursion.

34
00:05:11,383 --> 00:05:16,973
Write the function for List from the first alternative. Its first action is to call List.

35
00:05:17,423 --> 00:05:24,505
That call does not read a token. So the new call sees the same lookahead, i d, and calls List again.

36
00:05:24,955 --> 00:05:38,971
The call stack grows, and no input is consumed. The parser never stops. Both alternatives also start with i d, so the predict sets overlap. A left recursive grammar is not L L one.

37
00:05:39,421 --> 00:05:44,456
Here is the pattern from the notes. Goal goes to Goal Suffix, or to Prefix.

38
00:05:44,906 --> 00:05:56,042
What strings does Goal make? It must end with the Prefix alternative, so it starts with a Prefix. So the solution starts with Prefix, then a new nonterminal, Tail.

39
00:05:56,492 --> 00:06:01,889
Tail goes to Suffix Tail, or to epsilon. Epsilon stops the repetition.

40
00:06:02,339 --> 00:06:13,049
Both grammars give a Prefix, then zero or more Suffixes. The new Tail is right recursive, and the parser reads a token before each recursive call.

41
00:06:13,499 --> 00:06:25,936
Apply the pattern to List. The Prefix is i d, and the Suffix is comma i d. So List goes to i d Tail, and Tail goes to comma i d Tail, or to epsilon.

42
00:06:26,386 --> 00:06:34,706
Derive i d comma i d comma i d. We start from List. The orange box is the leftmost nonterminal.

43
00:06:35,156 --> 00:06:38,121
List has one alternative: i d Tail.

44
00:06:38,571 --> 00:06:42,561
The lookahead is a comma, so Tail takes comma i d Tail.

45
00:06:43,011 --> 00:06:46,723
Another comma. Tail takes comma i d Tail again.

46
00:06:47,173 --> 00:07:02,575
Now the input is finished. Tail takes epsilon. Each choice used one token of lookahead. A python check found no overlap in the predict sets, and the parser agrees with the language on all strings up to length 7.

47
00:07:03,025 --> 00:07:11,388
Compare the parse trees for i d comma i d comma i d. The old grammar builds a tree that leans to the left.

48
00:07:11,838 --> 00:07:17,577
The new grammar builds a tree that leans to the right. Each Tail holds the rest of the list.

49
00:07:18,027 --> 00:07:24,469
So the two grammars accept the same strings, but they build different trees. They are weakly equivalent.

50
00:07:24,919 --> 00:07:43,074
This matters for operators. Video 18 showed that a left associative operator, such as minus, needs left recursion. If we remove the left recursion, we also change the associativity. The notes say there is no simple solution in B N F.

51
00:07:43,524 --> 00:07:53,551
The notes suggest E B N F with repetition. The parser reads the repetition with a loop, and it can build a left associative tree from it.

52
00:07:54,001 --> 00:08:02,939
This is the E B N F grammar for the F language from the notes, Figure 4.5. It uses two extra notations.

53
00:08:03,389 --> 00:08:10,216
The star means repetition: zero or more. It appears three times, in Program, Expr and Term.

54
00:08:10,666 --> 00:08:23,509
The bar means alternation. It appears in Factor and Constant. B N F has neither the star nor the bar. We convert to B N F so that we can compute the sets for the full test.

55
00:08:23,959 --> 00:08:31,361
Take Expr. It is a Term, then plus Term, repeated zero or more times. The starred group is in orange.

56
00:08:31,811 --> 00:08:36,121
Replace the starred group with a new nonterminal. Call it TermTail.

57
00:08:36,571 --> 00:08:44,699
TermTail goes to plus Term TermTail, which is one more repetition. Or it goes to epsilon, which stops the repetition.

58
00:08:45,149 --> 00:08:50,034
So Expr still makes a Term, followed by zero or more plus Term groups.

59
00:08:50,484 --> 00:08:56,265
The bar is easy. Write each alternative as its own production with the same left side.

60
00:08:56,715 --> 00:09:05,526
Here is the full B N F grammar, Figure 4.6 in the notes. It has 17 productions and no star or bar.

61
00:09:05,976 --> 00:09:14,360
Three new nonterminals come from the three stars. FList, TermTail and FactorTail. Each has an epsilon production.

62
00:09:14,810 --> 00:09:30,021
We checked the conversion in python. A recognizer written from the E B N F and the B N F grammar agree on every token string up to length 6. Video 19 b uses this grammar for the full test.

63
00:09:30,471 --> 00:09:44,956
Now an exercise from the notes. This grammar is not suitable for a top down predictive parser. Fix the problems, then make the L L one parsing table. The terminals are the letters a, b and c.

64
00:09:45,406 --> 00:09:47,049
Pause the video and try it.

65
00:09:47,499 --> 00:09:55,797
Look for the two simple problems. R goes to R b c. The right side starts with R, so this is left recursion.

66
00:09:56,247 --> 00:10:02,797
Q goes to b b c, and Q goes to b c. Both start with b. That is a common prefix.

67
00:10:03,247 --> 00:10:10,479
The two L productions are fine. R starts with a or c. Q starts with b. They do not overlap.

68
00:10:10,929 --> 00:10:21,275
Fix the left recursion first. R has two non recursive alternatives, a b a and c a b a. Each is a Prefix. The Suffix is b c.

69
00:10:21,725 --> 00:10:31,965
So R goes to a b a R 1, or to c a b a R 1. R 1 goes to b c R 1, or to epsilon. The notes call it R prime.

70
00:10:32,415 --> 00:10:40,351
Now left factor Q. The common prefix is b. Q goes to b Qtail. Qtail goes to b c, or to c.

71
00:10:40,801 --> 00:10:50,017
The L productions do not change. A python check confirms that the new grammar gives the same strings as the old one, up to length 10.

72
00:10:50,467 --> 00:11:07,342
The table has a row for each nonterminal and a column for each lookahead token. A cell holds the production to use. For L: R starts with a or c, so those columns take L to R a. The b column takes L to Q b a.

73
00:11:07,792 --> 00:11:14,811
For R: lookahead a takes the a b a alternative. Lookahead c takes the c a b a alternative.

74
00:11:15,261 --> 00:11:34,525
For R 1: lookahead b takes b c R 1. When does R 1 go to epsilon? When the next token can follow R. In L to R a, an a follows R. So the a column takes R 1 to epsilon. Video 19 b explains this rule, the follow set.

75
00:11:34,975 --> 00:11:41,289
Q has one production, for lookahead b. Qtail: b takes b c, and c takes c.

76
00:11:41,739 --> 00:11:57,099
No cell holds two productions. So the new grammar is L L one. This table is our solution; the notes give only the grammar. A table driven parser built from it agrees with the language on every string up to length 10.

77
00:11:57,549 --> 00:12:11,395
Next exercise. Refactor this grammar to L L one form. S is an identifier with a list in parentheses, or in square brackets. A is a list of identifiers separated by commas.

78
00:12:11,845 --> 00:12:13,487
Pause the video and try it.

79
00:12:13,937 --> 00:12:20,999
There are two problems. The S alternatives have the common prefix i d. And A is left recursive.

80
00:12:21,449 --> 00:12:31,305
Left factor S. S goes to i d T. T goes to open parenthesis A close parenthesis, or to open bracket A close bracket.

81
00:12:31,755 --> 00:12:47,435
Remove the left recursion from A, with the same pattern as List. A goes to i d U. U goes to comma i d U, or to epsilon. A python check: same strings up to length 9, and no overlapping predict sets.

82
00:12:47,885 --> 00:12:58,338
Last exercise. The start symbol is expr, and i d is any single letter. Explain why an L L one parser cannot parse this grammar.

83
00:12:58,788 --> 00:13:00,431
Pause the video and try it.

84
00:13:00,881 --> 00:13:07,665
The problem is in expr. The first production starts with i d. Now follow the second production.

85
00:13:08,115 --> 00:13:10,291
expr goes to term term tail.

86
00:13:10,741 --> 00:13:12,917
term goes to factor factor tail.

87
00:13:13,367 --> 00:13:18,956
factor can go to i d. So the second production can also start with i d.

88
00:13:19,406 --> 00:13:37,219
Both expr productions can start with i d. It is a common prefix, but it is hidden behind three nonterminals. With lookahead i d, the parser cannot choose. A python check of the predict sets finds exactly this one overlap, on i d.

89
00:13:37,669 --> 00:13:47,824
To summarize. L L one means left to right, leftmost derivation, one token of lookahead. One token must predict the alternative.

90
00:13:48,274 --> 00:13:56,637
A common prefix blocks the choice. Left factor it: keep the prefix, and move the suffixes into a new Tail nonterminal.

91
00:13:57,087 --> 00:14:09,097
Left recursion makes recursive descent loop. Rewrite Goal as Prefix Tail, with Tail going to Suffix Tail or epsilon. The strings stay the same, but the trees change.

92
00:14:09,547 --> 00:14:20,747
To convert E B N F to B N F, split each bar into productions, and replace each star with a new nonterminal that uses recursion and epsilon.

93
00:14:21,197 --> 00:14:36,301
Look for prefixes that hide behind nonterminals. And remember: the simple tests can only show that a grammar is not L L one. The next video gives the full test: nullable, first, follow and predict sets.
